speechtext:
1.	(a) Subsidies are provided by the Department of Fisheries and Forestry in the fishing industry for fishermen and fishing firms towards the construction of wooden fishing vessels to the extent of either 30 per cent, 40 per cent, or 50 per cent of the cost of construction. Payments to the fishermen and firms in this respect for 1969-70 are estimated at $565,000; (b) In the fishing segment support is given to fishermen and fish processors, (i) through purchase and disposal of groundfish products, canned mackerel, salt cod, and Lake Erie yellow perch; (ii) through the provision of loans to companies engaged in processing groundfish products. Probable loans in 196970 are estimated at $4,200,000; (iii) through re-settlement of families in Newfoundland. Payments in this respect are estimated at $1,400,000 for 1969-70; (c) In the forestry segment, support of research in universities and other forestry activities is provided through a number of grants to forestry schools and to forestry organizations. Support is also given to the Province of Newfoundland under a 1965 agreement to assist with an inventory and land capability study. For 1969-70 the grants to Universities will amount to $283,000, and assistance to the Province of Newfoundland is estimated at $800,000. Grants to forestry organizations will be as follows: Canadian Forestry Association, $50,000; Commonwealth Forestry Institute (3,000 pounds Sterling), $8,000; British Columbia, Festival of Forestry Organization, $10,000.
2.	The following amounts were paid for subsidies and support programs during the years 1960-68: 1960-61, $4,605,511; 1961-62, $7,699,537; 1962-63, $9,043,003; 1963-64, $9,326,596; 1964-65,	$10,034,543;	1965-66,	$12,193,065;
1966-67, $11,731,104; 1967-68, $8,817,611; 196869, $9,429,551.
